            0.225
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the speed of light (unlike EM waves, sound waves, which you studied in Chapter 10, Sound Waves: Characteristics and Applications, are mechanical waves and require a medium to travel). The speed of light in vacuum is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 \\times 1 0 ^ { 8 } \\; \\mathrm { m s ^ { - 1 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . EM waves cover a wide range of frequencies, from high frequency or short wavelength radiation ("
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "gamma rays",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "X-rays",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ") to low frequency or long wavelength radiation "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(infrared",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "radio waves)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The high frequency EM waves, such as gamma rays and X-rays have very high energy and can be harmful for life on Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.045,
            0.233,
            0.506,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.045,
                0.233,
                0.506,
                0.424
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.143,
                0.429,
                0.41,
                0.446
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.2: Electromagnetic spectrum"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.517,
            0.226,
            0.89,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The entire range of electromagnetic radiation called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "electromagnetic spectrum",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is shown in Fig. 13.2. However, the solar radiation reaching Earth is concentrated mainly in the ultraviolet (UV), visible and infrared (IR) range — about 99 per cent of the Sun’s energy falls within these wavelengths. These three regions of the spectrum shape the Earth’s climate and support life. Gamma rays and X-rays are mostly filtered by the Earth’s upper atmosphere, while microwaves and radio waves carry very little energy to significantly warm the Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.466,
            0.891,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Short wavelength UV radiation is mostly absorbed by the ozone layer in the upper atmosphere, protecting life and contributing to some atmospheric heating. Visible light from the Sun reaches the Earth’s surface and provides energy for photosynthesis, which is the primary source of food for most organisms. It also partly warms the land and water. Infrared radiation warms the Earth’s surface, which then re-radiates this heat back into the atmosphere. A portion of this outgoing heat is trapped by the greenhouse gases, such as carbon dioxide "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( \\dot { \\mathrm { C O } } _ { 2 } ) _ { \\mathrm { s } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , methane "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( \\mathrm { C H } _ { 4 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and water vapour, keeping the Earth warm enough to support life."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.049,
            0.616,
            0.112,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.049,
                0.616,
                0.112,
                0.682
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.628,
            0.328,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.656,
            0.866,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The UV rays lie in the wavelength range of 100 nm to 400 nm [1 nanometre (nm) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "= 1 0 ^ { - 9 }   \\mathrm { m } ]"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and have a much higher energy than the visible light. Prolonged exposure to these rays can damage the eyes and skin, and increase the risk of cancer. Therefore, people should use UV protective glasses and sunscreen if they are likely to be exposed to UV rays. UV rays are also useful in killing germs in water purifiers and help power fluorescent lights."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.777,
            0.891,
            0.931
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The amount of the Sun’s radiation that reaches the Earth’s surface is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "insolation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It is responsible for warming the Earth’s surface and its atmosphere. The average amount of solar energy received per unit time per unit area that is perpendicular to the Sun’s rays at the top of the Earth’s atmosphere is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "solar constant",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Its value is approximately 1.4 kilowatts per square metre (1.4 kWm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "–2",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "), or about 1400 joules per second per square metre "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 1 4 0 0 \\mathrm { ~ J ~ } \\mathrm { s ^ { - 1 } m ^ { - 2 } } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . It represents the Sun’s energy available on the Earth before any absorption, scattering or reflection occurs in the atmosphere."
            }
          ]
        },
